# A REVERSIBLE BLOCK CELLULAR AUTOMATON, the discrete substrate underneath every quantum cellular automaton, modeled and
# proved reversible by the kernel. A block (Margolus-neighborhood) automaton partitions the lattice into disjoint blocks
# and updates each block by one fixed LOCAL PERMUTATION of the block states. When that local rule is a bijection of the
# finite block alphabet, the whole automaton is reversible: from the state after a beat the state before it is recovered
# exactly, so no information is lost. This is the discrete analogue of unitary evolution, which is why reversible block
# rules are the classical skeleton a quantum cellular automaton is quantized from.
#
# The block here is THREE binary cells in a row, eight block states. The forward rule `step` is the cyclic rotation that
# carries each cell to its right neighbor, a permutation of order three (not order two), so reversibility cannot come from
# self-composition and must be witnessed by an explicit inverse `unstep`, the opposite rotation. The rule is genuinely a
# permutation: it moves states (shown by a concrete refutation), it loses nothing (both round-trip laws), and it conserves
# the particle COUNT (the number of on cells), the discrete conservation law that makes the dynamics physical.
load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag

# A cell, the smallest piece of the lattice: off or on, an absent or present particle.
form cell
  case off
  case on

# A block, the unit the local rule acts on: three cells in a row. The Margolus partition tiles the lattice with these
# disjoint blocks, and one fixed permutation of the eight block states updates every block at once.
form block
  case row
    link one, like cell
    link two, like cell
    link three, like cell

# The particle COUNT of a block, the conserved charge: how many of the three cells are on, a value from none to all.
# A reversible rule that permutes block states within each count class can never create or destroy particles.
form count
  case none
  case one
  case two
  case all

# THE FORWARD RULE, the local block permutation: a cyclic rotation that sends each cell to the position of its right
# neighbor. The block (a, b, c) becomes (c, a, b). This is a bijection of the eight block states, the order-three rotation
# that drives the automaton one beat forward.
task step
  take b, like block
  like block
  fork case, read b
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        make row
          bind one
            read three
          bind two
            read one
          bind three
            read two

# THE INVERSE RULE, the permutation run backward: the opposite rotation, sending each cell to the position of its left
# neighbor. The block (a, b, c) becomes (b, c, a). Because the forward rotation has order three rather than two, the
# inverse is a DIFFERENT rule, not the forward rule reused. Applying it after a beat undoes that beat exactly.
task unstep
  take b, like block
  like block
  fork case, read b
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        make row
          bind one
            read two
          bind two
            read three
          bind three
            read one

# TEMPLATE: one row of the count table, keyed on the first cell. For a fixed first cell give the resulting count against
# each of the four states of the remaining two cells, written off-off, off-on, on-off, on-on. One template fuses both
# halves of the eight-state count function below.
tree count-row
  take head
  take off-off
  take off-on
  take on-off
  take on-on

  hook fuse
    case {head}
      fork case, read two
        case off
          fork case, read three
            case off
              send back
                make {off-off}
            case on
              send back
                make {off-on}
        case on
          fork case, read three
            case off
              send back
                make {on-off}
            case on
              send back
                make {on-on}

# THE PARTICLE COUNT of a block, the charge the rule conserves: the number of on cells across the three positions, read
# off the eight block states by the template. This is the discrete quantity the permutation never changes.
task charge
  take b, like block
  like count
  fork case, read b
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      fork case, read one
        fuse count-row, off, none, one, one, two
        fuse count-row, on, one, two, two, all

# THE RULE IS REVERSIBLE, forward then backward: unstep (step b) = b, for every block b. Running the inverse rotation after
# the forward rotation returns the original block, so the beat loses no information. Proved by exhaustive induction over the
# block, all eight states resolving by computation. This is the discrete analogue of unitarity, the heart of the model.
rule unstep-undoes-step
  mark b, like block
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call step, read b
        link unstep
      read b
  fold b

# THE RULE IS REVERSIBLE, backward then forward: step (unstep b) = b, for every block b. The inverse really is a two-sided
# inverse, so the permutation is a genuine bijection of the eight block states in both directions.
rule step-undoes-unstep
  mark b, like block
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call unstep, read b
        link step
      read b
  fold b

# THE RULE CONSERVES THE PARTICLE COUNT: charge (step b) = charge b, for every block b. The forward rotation only moves
# particles between cells, it never creates or destroys one, so the number of on cells is the same before and after the
# beat. This is the conservation law that makes the dynamics physical. Proved by induction over all eight block states.
rule step-conserves-charge
  mark b, like block
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call charge
        call step, read b
      call charge, read b
  fold b

# THE INVERSE ALSO CONSERVES THE COUNT: charge (unstep b) = charge b. Both directions of time preserve the same charge,
# as a reversible conservation law must.
rule unstep-conserves-charge
  mark b, like block
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call charge
        call unstep, read b
      call charge, read b
  fold b

# The first cell of a block, a projection used to witness that the rule moves states.
task head-cell
  take b, like block
  like cell
  fork case, read b
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        read one

# THE RULE IS NOT THE IDENTITY: it genuinely moves states. On the block (on, off, off) the forward rotation yields
# (off, on, off), so the first cell goes from on to off. Reading the first cell after the beat gives off, not on, two
# distinct cell states, so the rule cannot be the identity. A reversible rule that did nothing would be trivial; this one
# truly stirs the lattice while losing no information.
rule step-is-not-identity
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call head-cell
        call step
          make row
            bind one
              make on
            bind two
              make off
            bind three
              make off
      make on
  calm miss
